//! The console, and the ways a run ends badly.
//!
//! The formatting half of a diagnostic (a string one scalar at a time, a
//! machine word as fixed-width hex) plus the reports that stop the machine:
//! `fatal` for a trap the kernel cannot survive, `fatal_kernel` for a broken
//! kernel invariant, `fatal_image` for a root image it refuses to boot and
//! `fatal_deadlock` for a system that can make no more progress. And the
//! console claim, which every report consults before it writes.
//!
//! It calls nothing in the kernel, which is what lets every other seam report.
//! The reports that tear a process down are not here: they own kernel state and
//! live in `sched`. The difference is whether there is anything left to clean
//! up.

import hal
import sosrt.{AbortCode, ascii_byte, hex_digit_of, hex_digits_per_word}

/// The kernel's console: the arch-free half of writing a diagnostic.
///
/// It formats (a string one scalar at a time, a machine word as fixed-width
/// hex) and hands each byte to the HAL, which knows what device it is.
/// `write_hex` is the same code on every target; only where the byte lands
/// differs.
///
/// # The UART handover protocol
///
/// There is one console UART and both the kernel and a driver process can drive
/// it. Unstated, that is a flaky-test mystery: two writers on one device
/// interleave mid-word and nothing in either is wrong. Ownership has three
/// phases:
///
///   1. **Boot.** The kernel owns the device outright. It narrates the banner,
///      the image, and anything that goes wrong.
///   2. **Handover.** `start_process` writes one marker line, `SOS: console
///      handover`, as the last thing the kernel says on its own initiative.
///      After it, the process owns the device.
///   3. **Reclaim, on diagnostics only.** The kernel writes again exactly where
///      the run has already failed or ended: a fault report, a process
///      teardown, a panic, or an interrupt on a line nothing is bound to. The
///      interleaving those may cause is acceptable because there is no longer a
///      transcript to protect.
///
/// A process that drives the device itself goes further and claims the
/// console (see "The console claim" below): while the claim stands the third
/// phase is suspended, and only a stop of the machine writes.
///
/// What the kernel writes on demand does not violate this: a
/// `SystemOp.DebugPrint` is the process asking the kernel to place a byte, and
/// the process sequences it. The protocol forbids the kernel narrating over a
/// process that is using the device.
///
/// A harness kernel that turns the tick report on (`enable_tick_report`) is the
/// one bounded exception: `TICK_REPORT_LIMIT` ticks are narrated after handover
/// before the tick path goes quiet. That is why the preemption case does not
/// assert its root's banner, and why a case whose transcript matters after
/// handover boots an image whose boot flags turn the tick off. The shipping
/// kernel never reports a tick.
///
/// The device half (which registers, and what a second writer does to them) is
/// stated in each HAL's console section.
public struct Console {
    /// A console is a capability to write, not a device handle: the HAL owns
    /// the device. The field records which sink the writes go to (there is
    /// one), since a zero-field struct has no reason to exist either.
    sink: UInt8,
}

extension Console {
    /// Write one byte.
    func write_byte(&self, b: UInt8) {
        hal.console_byte(b)
    }

    /// Write an ASCII string, byte by byte. The scalar-to-byte rule is
    /// `sosrt`'s, so the kernel console and a process console agree on what a
    /// non-ASCII scalar becomes.
    ///
    /// No preemption point, and this is the one verdict that accepts latency
    /// rather than arguing it away. The loop is bounded by the device drain, so
    /// a long diagnostic line is masked latency. Two reasons not to point it:
    /// half of this module's callers are the fault reports, which run with the
    /// kernel's state already deliberately inconsistent; and a delivery here
    /// would interleave the tick path's own console output into the middle of
    /// the line being written, into the transcript the acceptance oracle diffs.
    public func write_str(&self, msg: String) {
        for scalar in msg.scalars() {
            hal.console_byte(ascii_byte(scalar))
        }
    }

    /// Write a machine word as `0x` + one hex digit per nibble. No allocation,
    /// no formatting machinery.
    ///
    /// The width is the target's word, not a constant: eight digits on a 32-bit
    /// target and sixteen on a 64-bit one. A fixed eight would print the low
    /// half of a 64-bit address and look like a complete answer.
    public func write_hex(&self, v: UInt) {
        self.write_str("0x")
        let digits = hex_digits_per_word()
        var n = 0
        while n < digits {
            hal.console_byte(hex_digit_of(v, n))
            n = n + 1
        }
    }
}

/// A console for this board. Constructing one costs nothing.
public func console() -> Console {
    Console(sink: 0)
}

// =============================================================================
// The console claim (spec §9a)
// =============================================================================
//
// A process that drives the console's device itself claims the console
// (`SystemOp.ConsoleClaim`), and while the claim stands the kernel writes
// nothing to it but what a stop writes. Each line the kernel would have
// written about another process is held back and counted, and the debug print
// is refused and counted; when the console comes back, at a release or at the
// claimant's death, one line says what was held, so a line nobody printed is
// never lost without a trace. A line about the claimant itself comes from its
// fault or its end, so the claim is ended first and the line is written.
//
// The claim is decided per line, at the line's first byte, and every line is
// written inside one kernel entry with no preemption point, as are the claim,
// the release and the teardown that ends a claim. So no line is ever half on
// each side of a claim.
//
// It is state the kernel keeps at the bottom of its module order so that every
// report can consult it, and the HAL's own stops reach it through
// `sos_console_stop`. It names the claimant by its record and its pid, which
// the callers pass: this module reads no kernel table.

/// Who holds the console, and what the kernel held back while they did.
struct ConsoleClaim {
    /// The claimant's record, plus one; 0 is no claim.
    holder: Int,
    /// The claimant's pid, so that a reused record is never taken for it.
    pid: UInt64,
    /// The lines the kernel did not write while the claim stood.
    lines_held: UInt,
    /// The debug-print bytes refused while the claim stood.
    bytes_refused: UInt,
}

unsafe static var CONSOLE_CLAIM: ConsoleClaim = ConsoleClaim(
    holder: 0, pid: 0, lines_held: 0, bytes_refused: 0)

/// Whether a process holds the console.
public func console_claimed() unsafe -> Bool {
    CONSOLE_CLAIM.holder != 0
}

/// Whether process `p`, whose pid is `pid`, holds the console.
public func console_held_by(p: Int, pid: UInt64) unsafe -> Bool {
    CONSOLE_CLAIM.holder == p + 1 && CONSOLE_CLAIM.pid == pid
}

/// Give the console to process `p`, whose pid is `pid`. The caller has found
/// it unclaimed.
public func console_claim(p: Int, pid: UInt64) unsafe {
    CONSOLE_CLAIM.holder = p + 1
    CONSOLE_CLAIM.pid = pid
}

/// End the claim and take the console back, writing the held-back line when
/// anything was held.
///
/// The claim is cleared before anything is written, so a stop reached from
/// inside the write finds the console already back and writes as it would.
public func console_return() unsafe {
    let held = CONSOLE_CLAIM.lines_held
    let refused = CONSOLE_CLAIM.bytes_refused
    CONSOLE_CLAIM = ConsoleClaim(holder: 0, pid: 0, lines_held: 0,
                                 bytes_refused: 0)
    write_returned(held, refused)
}

/// The held-back line, when anything was held.
func write_returned(held: UInt, refused: UInt) {
    if held == 0 && refused == 0 {
        return
    }
    let uart = console()
    uart.write_str("SOS: console returned, lines held=")
    uart.write_hex(held)
    uart.write_str(", debug bytes refused=")
    uart.write_hex(refused)
    uart.write_str("\n")
}

/// Take the console back for a stop of the machine: a panic, a fault the
/// kernel cannot survive, root's end, or one of the HAL's own stops. With no
/// claim it does nothing.
///
/// The claim is ended over the claimant's head, the console's line is masked
/// at the controller (`hal.console_reclaim`), so the claimant's interrupt
/// stays quiet whatever the stop does next, and the held-back line is
/// written. No FIFO is reset: the bytes the claimant stored are the run's last
/// output and go out first, and the stop's lines follow them.
///
/// The HAL's stops reach it by symbol, from below the module order. The claim
/// is cleared first, so a stop reached from inside this one finds no claim.
@export("sos_console_stop")
public func console_stop() unsafe {
    if CONSOLE_CLAIM.holder == 0 {
        return
    }
    let held = CONSOLE_CLAIM.lines_held
    let refused = CONSOLE_CLAIM.bytes_refused
    CONSOLE_CLAIM = ConsoleClaim(holder: 0, pid: 0, lines_held: 0,
                                 bytes_refused: 0)
    hal.console_reclaim()
    write_returned(held, refused)
}

/// Whether the kernel may write a line now: true with no claim; false while
/// one stands, counting the line as held back. A line about the claimant
/// itself, or a stop's, ends the claim before it asks.
public func console_line() unsafe -> Bool {
    if CONSOLE_CLAIM.holder == 0 {
        return true
    }
    CONSOLE_CLAIM.lines_held = CONSOLE_CLAIM.lines_held &+ 1
    false
}

/// Whether the debug print must refuse its byte, counting it when it does: a
/// claim refuses every caller's, the claimant's included.
public func console_refuses_byte() unsafe -> Bool {
    if CONSOLE_CLAIM.holder == 0 {
        return false
    }
    CONSOLE_CLAIM.bytes_refused = CONSOLE_CLAIM.bytes_refused &+ 1
    true
}

/// Exit codes the kernel reports through the platform's failure channel,
/// distinct from any status root chooses for itself.
///
/// A type rather than a family of `UInt`s so `fatal` cannot be handed an
/// arbitrary number: the kernel's failure vocabulary is closed, and the harness
/// asserts on these values.
///
/// 3 is deliberately absent. The values are per-case, so a gap costs nothing,
/// and reusing a retired number would make unrelated transcripts read alike.
public enum ExitCode: UInt {
    case UmodeFault = 2,
    case BadImage = 4,
    case ProcessFault = 5,
    /// The kernel found its own invariant broken: a state the kernel's own
    /// rules say cannot exist. Not a process's mistake and not the build's. It
    /// is a separate number from `BadImage` because the two say opposite things
    /// about who is at fault.
    case KernelBug = 6,
    /// Every thread is blocked and nothing outside the thread set can wake one
    /// (spec §7). The system's design is at fault, not a process, so no
    /// process is ended and none is named.
    case Deadlock = 7,
    /// Root was ended by another process through `ProcessOp.Kill`. The killer's
    /// code is not the machine's status: a root that exits with a number reads
    /// as that number, so a killed root's status is a code of its own, and it
    /// is not a clean finish whatever code the killer passed.
    case KilledRoot = 8,
    /// Root ended itself through `PANIC_OP`. It is the status a panic in the
    /// kernel stops the machine with too (`sosrt`'s `AbortCode.Panic`, held
    /// equal below), so a panic reads the same whichever side raised it.
    case Panic = 64,
}

static_assert((ExitCode.Panic as UInt) == ((AbortCode.Panic as UInt32) as UInt),
              "a root's panic and a kernel's panic stop the machine alike")

// =============================================================================
// Fault reporting
// =============================================================================

/// Report a fatal condition on the console and fail the run. Never returns: a
/// fault is diagnosed and the machine stops non-zero, never a hang.
public func fatal(tag: String, cause: UInt, epc: UInt, tval: UInt, code: ExitCode) {
    console_stop()
    let uart = console()
    uart.write_str("SOS: fault ")
    uart.write_str(tag)
    uart.write_str(" cause=")
    uart.write_hex(cause)
    uart.write_str(" epc=")
    uart.write_hex(epc)
    uart.write_str(" tval=")
    uart.write_hex(tval)
    uart.write_str("\n")
    hal.exit_fail(code as UInt)
}

/// Stop: the kernel broke its own invariant. Never returns.
///
/// The line between the three failure vocabularies is whose mistake it was. A
/// caller error ends the process (`fault_process`); a malformed boot image ends
/// the machine as `fatal_image`, because the build wrote those bytes; and this
/// one ends the machine because the kernel reached a state its own rules say is
/// unreachable. Reporting it as either of the others would blame somebody who
/// did nothing wrong.
///
/// Examples: a `map()` that meets the physical row wall with quota headroom
/// left (`install_quota_limits` is what makes it unreachable), and a
/// `free_object` reaching a kind that has no slab to free into.
public func fatal_kernel(reason: String, detail: UInt) {
    console_stop()
    let uart = console()
    uart.write_str("SOS: kernel bug: ")
    uart.write_str(reason)
    uart.write_str(" (")
    uart.write_hex(detail)
    uart.write_str(")\n")
    hal.exit_fail(ExitCode.KernelBug as UInt)
}

/// Stop: every thread is blocked and nothing can wake one. Never returns.
///
/// The report names no process. Which thread blocked last is an accident of
/// scheduling, so a line that carried it would differ from run to run, and the
/// kernel attempts no recovery: nothing is ended and nothing is torn down. The
/// exit code is its own, so a harness tells this stop from a process fault.
public func fatal_deadlock() {
    console_stop()
    let uart = console()
    uart.write_str("SOS: deadlock: every thread is blocked and nothing can ")
    uart.write_str("wake one\n")
    hal.exit_fail(ExitCode.Deadlock as UInt)
}

/// Reject the root image. Never returns.
public func fatal_image(reason: String, detail: UInt) {
    console_stop()
    let uart = console()
    uart.write_str("SOS: bad root image: ")
    uart.write_str(reason)
    uart.write_str(" (")
    uart.write_hex(detail)
    uart.write_str(")\n")
    hal.exit_fail(ExitCode.BadImage as UInt)
}
